    "content": "The Houthi movement in Yemen announced on Tuesday that it had carried out new attacks targeting a Saudi oil refinery and an economic facility in the capital, Riyadh. In a statement released by the group's media office, the Houthis described the strikes as retaliatory operations against Saudi Arabia. The statement did not specify the exact time of the attacks or provide details on the extent of damage or casualties. Saudi authorities have not yet commented on the claims, and independent verification was not immediately possible.\n\nThe Houthis, who have been fighting a Saudi-led coalition since 2015, have frequently launched missile and drone attacks on Saudi territory, including oil infrastructure and airports. The conflict in Yemen began when the Houthis seized the capital, Sanaa, in 2014, prompting the Saudi-led intervention to restore the internationally recognized government. The war has killed tens of thousands of people and created one of the world's worst humanitarian crises. Previous attacks on Saudi oil facilities, such as the 2019 strike on Abqaiq and Khurais, briefly disrupted global oil supplies and heightened regional tensions.\n\nSaudi Arabia has not confirmed the latest attacks, and there has been no immediate report of damage or disruption to oil production. The kingdom has previously downplayed the impact of Houthi strikes, while the Houthis have often exaggerated their claims. The situation remains fluid, and further details may emerge. The international community has repeatedly called for de-escalation and a negotiated end to the Yemen conflict.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant due to the close economic and security ties between Somalia and Saudi Arabia. Many Somalis rely on remittances from family members working in Saudi Arabia, and any disruption to the Saudi economy or regional stability could affect those flows. Additionally, the Yemen conflict has directly impacted Somalia through arms smuggling, refugee flows, and maritime security threats in the Gulf of Aden. Escalating Houthi attacks could further destabilize the region and complicate ongoing peace efforts in Somalia.\n\nSomali traders with business interests in Saudi Arabia and the broader Gulf region may also face uncertainty. The Somali government has maintained a neutral stance on the Yemen conflict, balancing its relationships with Saudi Arabia and Iran. The diaspora community in Saudi Arabia, which numbers in the hundreds of thousands, could be affected by heightened security measures. Observers note that any major escalation in the Red Sea region could also impact shipping routes used for Somali exports, including livestock.\n\nThe Houthis have recently intensified their attacks on Saudi Arabia and the UAE, another member of the Saudi-led coalition. The group has also targeted airports and military bases in Saudi cities. The latest claim comes as peace talks brokered by the United Nations have stalled. The international community continues to urge restraint and dialogue to prevent a wider regional conflagration.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/xuutiyiinta-oo-sheegay-in-ay-weerareen-warshadda-saliidda-ee-sacuudiga-iyo-xarunta-dhaqaalaha-ee-riyaad/427995/",
